export const AUTHORING_GUIDE_MARKDOWN = "# RPGCode program authoring\n\n**TOOL-10 Program editing (story 11.7, #1705; editor-features T-40).** *In plain terms:* an author can open a `.prg`, see it coloured as they type, jump to its functions, labels and comments, see the shape of the command they are typing, insert a command from a list, start a new program from a header the game chooses, and hide everything but the text. It works the same in the browser editor (which shows the text in Monaco, [ADR 0021](../../adr/0021-monaco-code-editor.md)) and in VS Code (which shows it in its own text editor). None of it needs a syntax grammar from the later grammar epic, and if the language server fails the author can still type and save. *Precisely:* this entry fixes the pieces below; each is shared by both hosts where the host has the part, and no host keeps its own copy of the rules.\n- **Navigation (bookmarks).** `textDocument/documentSymbol` lists, in source order and as one flat list, the functions, classes, labels and comments of the open file. A **comment bookmark** is a comment that starts its line (the first non-blank text is `//`, `**`, `*` or `/*`); its name is the comment's text without the marker and without a closing `*/`, trimmed, and a comment with no letter or digit in it (a ruled line of `-----`) is not listed. A trailing comment after code is not listed. A block comment is listed once, by its first line. Symbols are reported with kind `Function`, `Class`, `Key` (a label) and `String` (a comment). The browser editor shows them as its bookmark list and VS Code shows them in the Outline.\n- **Call tips** are [the signature help above](#tool-1); the browser editor shows them in Monaco's parameter hints, VS Code in its own.\n- **Command reference and insertion.** The quick reference is a projection of the registered commands' documentation (`commandDocumentation`), computed by `editor-model` from the same catalogue as hover, and so needs no running language server. One entry per public name and alias: `name`, `canonicalName`, `aliasOf` (or `null`), `syntax` (the call as it is written, such as `mwin(text)`, with an optional parameter in brackets, a by-reference parameter prefixed `&` and a variadic one ending `...`), the one-sentence `summary`, and `groups`. **Insert** puts `name(` and the names of the **required** parameters, separated by `, `, and `)` at the cursor, replacing any selection; the editor selects the parameter text so the author overtypes it. A command with no required parameters inserts `name()` with the cursor between the brackets. Double-clicking an entry (the browser) or choosing it in the picker (VS Code) inserts it. Insertion is one undoable edit.\n- **Header template.** The preference `program.headerTemplate` (a string, user or project layer, at most 500 characters) is the text a new program starts with. `{file}` becomes the file name (`intro.prg`), `{name}` the file name without its extension, and `{path}` the project-relative path; any other `{…}` is left as written, so a template can never fail. The default is `// {file}` followed by a blank line. **New program** expands the template and opens the created file. It refuses a selected path when its case-insensitive existence check detects an existing file, with an “already exists” message, and creates nothing. A host **MUST** use a genuine atomic exclusive-create operation when its API supports one. The current browser and VS Code adapters instead check the directory listing and then call an ordinary write: an external actor can create the path between those steps, and that write may replace the concurrent file. This disclosed check/write race is accepted for those hosts; it is not an absolute no-overwrite guarantee. See [ADR 0035](../../adr/0035-program-new-no-overwrite-limits.md).\n- **Distraction-free mode** (the legacy \"Notepad mode\"). A command that hides the editor's panels, tab strip and toolbars and leaves only the text, and that a second use or Escape reverses. The browser editor hides its shell panels and the program toolbar; VS Code uses its Zen mode. Neither mode changes the document, the dirty state or the language service.\n- **Failure.** The language server is a helper to the text, never its owner. If it is missing, fails to start, stops, or answers an error, the document still edits, undoes, saves and reopens; the editor says in one status line that language features are unavailable, and the features that need the server (colouring, bookmarks, call tips, hover, diagnostics) simply show nothing. Header insertion, command insertion, undo and save do not use the server.\n- **VS Code extensions.** The language client stays in `extensions/rpgcode` ([TOOL-2](#tool-2)); this entry is the editor epic's answer to its question whether the editor host takes the client over, and the answer is no: two extensions keep two jobs. `extensions/rpgcode` turns semantic highlighting on for the `rpgcode` language and is otherwise unchanged. The editor extension (`tk-reawakened.editor`) adds the commands **New program**, **Insert header**, **Insert command** (a picker over the quick reference) and **Distraction-free mode**; they read the shared preferences and the shared command catalogue, never the language server, so they work when it is absent. The settings of the header template live with the other editor preferences.\n\nThis guide is drawn from the repository's TOOL-10 editor specification. It is a maintained authoring reference, not the legacy HTML manual.";
export const WHATS_NEW_MARKDOWN = "# What's New\n\n- New-project command tests compile their shared extension fixture once while keeping each case's workspace and module state isolated (#2798).\n\n- VS Code new-project creation normalizes selected folder paths and leaves the pending main-file open for the restarted host when a folder window becomes a workspace, even if an early folder event arrives. It resumes without requiring a file-search provider; the real web-workbench proof drives the destination picker and checks generated files and the opened editor (#2759, #2804, #2806, #2807, #2821).\n\n- Story 11.11 (#1709) connects the shared RPGCode quick reference, a repository-sourced authoring guide and current release notes to the workspace Help menu and command palette. F1 opens accessible descriptions for focused settings; help documents render repository content safely and return focus to their invoking field or command.\n\n- Program editor insertions, undo, redo and save now preserve their order; Monaco's context-menu history uses the shared document. Language-service errors and formatting refusals are shown without blocking editing (#2727–#2729).\n\n- Program editing preserves pending insertions and typing order across saves and view changes, shares language-document ownership across views, and keeps browser history and project context consistent between views. New programs refuse detected collisions in differently cased folders (#2732–#2738).\n\n- Pending Undo and Redo preserve subsequent typing, including repeated text and multi-cursor edits, even when the view closes; each Save keeps its requested revision and leaves later typing unsaved, and untouched mixed line endings survive rebasing. Bookmarks refresh across views, and new files use existing folder casing (#2741–#2745, #2747–#2749, #2753, #2754, #2756–#2758, #2760–#2765).\n\n- Story 11.7 (#1705; [TOOL-10](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-tooling.md#tool-10), T-40 in [editor features](docs/editor-features.md)) edits RPGCode programs in the browser editor in Monaco and in VS Code in its native text editor, with the same language server: live colouring (semantic tokens that never fail on unfinished text), an outline/bookmark list of functions, labels and line-leading comments, call tips, a command quick reference that inserts `name(params)`, a configurable header template (`program.headerTemplate`, New program, Insert header) and distraction-free mode. The browser program document preserves Windows-1252 bytes and each line ending; VS Code keeps its native `TextDocument` and normal line-ending behavior, with exact per-break preservation not required there (owner ruling recorded in TOOL-10; former blocker [#2718](https://github.com/tk-reawakened/rpgtoolkit-reawakened/issues/2718)). A language-server failure never stops editing or saving. Adds `language.connect()` to platform-services, the `program` document kind, Monaco 0.57.0 (pinned; worker and icon font audited in `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES`, [evidence](docs/findings/monaco-integration/README.md)) under the CSP of [ADR 0029](docs/adr/0029-monaco-csp-style-src.md), and a licence check for files a dependency ships. New program refuses a detected existing path with an \"already exists\" message; browser and VS Code disclose their accepted check/write race in which a concurrent external creation may be replaced, as recorded in [ADR 0035](docs/adr/0035-program-new-no-overwrite-limits.md).\n\n- New-project creation refuses conflicts in every component of a nested starter path and opens a project nested inside a VS Code workspace with the new project folder as its root (#2724, #2725; [new-project layout](docs/spec/formats/packaging-and-paths.md#new-project)).\n\n- New projects resume opening after VS Code restarts without unrelated windows consuming the request, and refuse starter catalogues with conflicting directory casing or file/directory aliases, including conflicts with the generated main file, before writing (#2730, #2731, #2739, #2740, #2746).\n\n- New-project creation refuses differently cased existing reserved folders rather than create starter assets that case-sensitive hosts cannot find (#2755).\n\n- Story 11.10 adds the new-project wizard (#1708; T-04 and T-05 in [editor features](docs/editor-features.md#reawakened-rules-for-the-new-project-wizard-story-1110), [layout, defaults and starter catalogue](docs/spec/formats/packaging-and-paths.md#new-project)). The browser editor's \"New project…\" shows one form and VS Code's \"RPG Toolkit: New project\" asks the same questions one at a time: game name, main file, starter contents and a destination folder. It writes the 14 reserved folders and `Main/<name>.gam` (minor 9) into that folder, which becomes the project root, and opens the main file. Starter contents are explicit and none is ticked by default: a start-up program, an empty start board, an empty first character (no artwork) and the built-in menu and battle names are available; default tiles, system programs and a music pack are listed as unavailable with their reason because no rights-cleared material exists, and are never replaced by a placeholder. Every path is checked against the destination first and an existing file is never replaced — an empty one included: a conflict is listed and nothing is written, and the check is a check (not an atomic create; see #2713). The main file is written last; a failure keeps what was written, deletes nothing, names any file the failed write may have left so the author can remove it, and says what is pending; cancelling or dismissing the folder dialog writes nothing. `platform-services` gains `fs.createFolder` so empty folders can exist, implemented by the browser, VS Code and in-memory hosts.\n\n- Story 12.9 adds package export in the browser editor and VS Code (#1719; [packaging and paths](docs/spec/formats/packaging-and-paths.md#distribution-package-this-projects-own)). Save the game, review the included and excluded files, and export a playable TPK with its matching seven-field install descriptor. Preflight excludes saved games, other main files, test specs, editor-only authoring files and `.tkr-history/`; it refuses detected links, unsafe paths and missing declared targets. VS Code confirms existing output names and exports to the reserved `.tkr-exports/` folder or outside the project. Locally chosen folders are trusted; this does not promise protection against malicious concurrent filesystem changes. Browser export uses two downloads and explains how to restore their matching names if the browser renames either file (#2660).\n\n[Read the full changelog](https://github.com/tk-reawakened/rpgtoolkit-reawakened/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md).";
